'''Adonia''', also called '''Orth''' or the '''World''', is the fifth planet from the star [[Aurelius]] and the largest terrestrial body in the Aurelius system. It is located at a mean distance of 1.05 AU from its host star, a luminous Type G0V yellow dwarf. Adonia is frequently referred to as the Turquoise Jewel due to the distinct copper-green tint of its global hydrosphere, a phenomenon caused by the high concentration of cupric minerals released by hydrothermal venting. This venting is driven by the gravitational influence of its massive planetary neighbor, [[Quetzal]].

Adonia formed approximately 4.2 billion years ago and supports a robust, diverse biosphere. The planet possesses a dense, nitrogen-oxygen atmosphere that sustains a hyper-tropical climate, with high global average temperatures maintained by the intense luminosity of Aurelius (approximately 1.35 times standard solar luminosity). The planet completes one revolution around its star every 371.25 local solar days. This specific orbital period forms the astronomical basis of the Adonian Calendar, which organizes time into three standard years of 371 days followed by a single leap year of 372 days.

A defining characteristic of the Adonian system is its 4:1 orbital resonance with the gas giant Quetzal, which orbits inward at 0.89 AU. Every four years, on the intercalary day of the leap year ('''February 31st'''), Quetzal reaches its inferior conjunction, passing directly between Adonia and the star. This event, known as the '''Day of the Dragon''', generates powerful gravitational "King Tides" (or Dragon Tides) that raise global sea levels by several meters for a 24-hour period. This cyclic tidal stress is the primary driver of the planet's tectonic activity, fueling the underwater vents that dye the oceans their characteristic turquoise color.

Adonia possesses two natural satellites, [[Imera]] and [[Theia]], which stabilize its axial tilt of 24.3 degrees and contribute to the daily tidal rhythm. The planet is home to approximately 8.5 billion humans organized into roughly 230 sovereign states. It serves as the biological and cultural anchor for all civilization in the Aurelius system.

==Name and etymology==
The Aetolian Ἀδώνια (Adōnia) was a borrowing from the Semitic word adon, meaning lord, which is related to Adonai, one of the names used to refer to the deity in the Hebrew texts and still used in Judaism to the present day. Humans started using this word to represent the planet through the Triskaidetheist faith in Aetolia. The term reflects the sense of renewal and divine providence attributed to the world by early settlers and theologians who viewed the planet's turquoise oceans as a sign of purity. Ancient Aetolian texts often used the term Gaia (Γαῖα) to refer to the personification of the world's soil and fertility.

==Composition and structure==
===Internal structure===
Adonia's interior, like that of other terrestrial planets, is divided into layers by their chemical or physical (rheological) properties. The outer layer is a chemically distinct silicate solid crust, which is underlain by a highly viscous solid mantle. The crust is separated from the mantle by the Mohorovicic discontinuity. The thickness of the crust varies from about 6 km under the oceans to 30 to 50 km for the continents. Adonia's mantle is approximately 2,900 km thick. The core-mantle boundary (Gutenberg discontinuity) separates the mantle from the core.

The core of Adonia is divided into two parts: a solid inner core with a radius of about 1,221 km and a liquid outer core extending beyond it to a radius of about 3,400 km. The liquid outer core is composed of iron and nickel with smaller amounts of lighter elements. Its convection generates Adonia's magnetic field through the dynamo effect. The solid inner core is believed to be composed primarily of an iron-nickel alloy. Seismic data indicates the inner core is rotating slightly faster than the rest of the planet. The magnetic field created by the core extends thousands of kilometers into space, forming the magnetosphere which deflects the stellar winds of Solarus.

===Pangaea===
Pangaea was a supercontinent that existed during the late Paleozoic and early Mesozoic eras, forming approximately 300 million years ago. It began to break apart around 200 million years ago. The single global ocean which surrounded Pangaea is accordingly named Panthalassa. The name Pangaea is derived from Ancient Aetolian pan (πᾶν) meaning entire, and Gaia (Γαῖα) meaning Mother Adonia. The name was coined during a 1927 symposium discussing the Bruellan Alfred Wegener's theory of continental drift. In his book The Origin of Continents and Oceans (Die Entstehung der Kontinente und Ozeane), first published in 1915, he postulated that prior to breaking up and drifting to their present locations, all the continents had at one time formed a single supercontinent which he called the Urkontinent.

Originally, this theory was rejected because the predominant theory was that Adonia was cooling and shrinking, with mountains being the last regions to shrink. Wegener's theory that mountains were made by two land masses colliding with each other seemed unlikely because it was thought that nothing could move a landmass as large as a continent. The forming of supercontinents and their breaking up appears to have been cyclical through Adonia's history. There may have been several others before Pangaea, such as Rodinia, Pannotia, and Columbia.

Fossil evidence for Pangaea includes the presence of similar and identical species on continents that are now great distances apart. For example, fossils of the therapsid Lystrosaurus have been found in Illypnia, Kaftia and Western Fosia, alongside members of the Glossopteris flora, whose distribution would have ranged from the polar circle to the equator if the continents had been in their present position; similarly, the freshwater reptile Mesosaurus has been found in only localized regions of the coasts of Abyala and Western Kaftia.

Additional evidence for Pangaea is found in the geology of adjacent continents, including matching geological trends between the eastern coast of the Northwest Continent and the western coast of Kaftia. The polar ice cap of the Carboniferous Period covered the southern end of Pangaea. Glacial deposits, specifically till, of the same age and structure are found on many separate continents which would have been together in the continent of Pangaea. This suggests a massive, unified climatic zone that existed before the continental drift initiated by mantle convection. The matching strata and coal deposits across these now-separated ranges serve as a geological fingerprint of the original unified landmass.

Paleomagnetic study of apparent polar wandering paths also support the theory of a super-continent. Geologists can determine the movement of continental plates by examining the orientation of magnetic minerals in rocks; when rocks are formed, they take on the magnetic properties of Adonia and indicate in which direction the poles lie relative to the rock. Samples of sedimentary rock and intrusive igneous rock have magnetic orientations that are typically an average of these secular variations in the orientation of Magnetic South because their magnetic fields were not formed in an instant. Magnetic differences between sample groups whose age varies by millions of years is due to a combination of true polar wander and the drifting of continents. The true polar wander component is identical for all samples, and can be removed, leaving geologists with the portion of this motion that shows continental drift and can be used to help reconstruct earlier continental positions. 

The continuity of mountain chains provides further evidence for Pangaea. One example of this is the Atlas Mountains chain which extends from the southwestern Fosia to the Kaskaskian range of Lurandia. The matching strata across these ranges indicate they were once part of a continuous orogenic belt.

===Tectonic plates===
The mechanically rigid outer layer of Adonia, the lithosphere, is broken into pieces called tectonic plates. These plates move in relation to one another at convergent boundaries, divergent boundaries, and transform boundaries. Earthquakes, volcanic activity, mountain-building, and oceanic trench formation can occur along these plate boundaries. The tectonic plates ride on top of the asthenosphere, the solid part of the upper mantle that can flow and move along with the plates, and their motion is strongly coupled with convection patterns inside Adonia's mantle.

As the tectonic plates migrate across the planet, the ocean floor is subducted under the leading edges of the plates at convergent boundaries. At the same time, the upwelling of mantle material at divergent boundaries creates mid-ocean ridges. The combination of these processes continually recycles the oceanic crust back into the mantle. Due to this recycling, most of the ocean floor is less than 100 myr old in age. The oldest oceanic crust is located in the Western Shendan, and has an estimated age of about 200 myr. By comparison, the oldest dated continental crust is 4,030 myr.

The nine major plates are the Shendan, Lurandian, Fosian, Almurian, XXX, Abyalan, XXX, XXX, and Lurandian. Other notable plates include the Trestan Plate, the Xtepla Plate, the Keko Plate and the Masnorte Plate. The North Fosian Plate fused with the South Fosian Plate between 50 and 55 mya. The fastest-moving plates are the oceanic plates, with the Keko Plate advancing at a rate of 75 mm/year and the Shendan Plate moving 52–69 mm/year. At the other extreme, the slowest-moving plate is the Lurandian Plate, progressing at a typical rate of about 21 mm/year. This tectonic activity is essential for the carbon cycle and long-term climate stability.

